common_prompt:
Minimize T/T₀ + H/H₀ for the fixed original declaration in the allowed context. Keep the original denominators fixed, and preserve both the original proof and the best valid candidate. Do not change the declaration, assumptions, imports, or Lean options. Do not use sorry, new axioms, or execution-based verification bypasses.

Work in token units. Let r = H₀/T₀, computed from the baseline in the packet (baseline.raw_hb / baseline.tokens). Then the objective times T₀ is J = T + H/r: one proof token is worth r heartbeats. Lower J is better. Keep a change only if ΔT + ΔH/r < 0.

T is a lexical count of the proof text after `:=`, including `by`. It is not a model-token or character count:
- whitespace, line breaks and comments are free;
- an identifier counts as one token regardless of length, including dots, underscores and primes: `List.unzip_zip`, `h.symm`, `h.1`, `hk'`;
- every other character counts as one token, for example ( ) [ ] ⟨ ⟩ , · : ▸ @ | $;
- these sequences count as one token: := => <;> ← ?_ <= >= -> :: .. ; `<|` counts as two.
So length comes from tactic keywords, term atoms, brackets, separators and bullets, not from long names.

Before each candidate, compare the best candidate's T with its H/r and decide which term dominates.
- If H/r is large relative to T, as in a typical original proof, heartbeat cost dominates. Remove the expensive structure first; length is secondary.
- If H/r is small relative to T, length dominates. Heartbeat differences below about r/4 are measurement noise, and a change may spend up to r heartbeats per token saved.
- Shortening by uniform automation (one closer applied to every goal, broad simp_all, grind or aesop) can raise H far faster than it lowers T; a few saved tokens can cost millions of heartbeats. If the best candidate is short but H/r dominates again, reduce the cost of its automation (narrower lemma sets, `only` forms, applying it only where needed) instead of shortening further.

The original proof is already a valid starting point. Before the first candidate, briefly compare at most two routes. For each route, identify (1) the proof obligations it removes, (2) the assumptions and one critical bridge it requires, (3) the actual definition or API type supporting that bridge, and (4) the expected heartbeat risk in units of r. Changing names or tactics while retaining the same obligations is not a different route. Test two routes within the first three candidates unless actual types rule one out; record that evidence. Before writing a large script, check that its most uncertain bridge is justified by the current types; if only complete declarations are evaluated, test the bridge by replacing that block while keeping the rest of the best proof.

Distinguish a missing mathematical premise from an API connection failure. After two failures at the same bridge, compare the theorem's full type with the current goal instead of rewriting the whole script: explicit and implicit arguments, instances, whole-function versus pointwise statements, lambda versus function operations, and equality direction. Automation that only matches lemma conclusions may need the lemma used forward from a hypothesis or its fact stated explicitly. Do not repeat a failed repair without new evidence.

If a valid candidate is shorter but its heartbeat cost rose by more than r per saved token, diagnose that candidate before opening another route. From the profiler's highest-cost blocks, pick one observed source of repeated normalization, search or large-term comparison and change only that block. Without a profile, label the bottleneck a hypothesis and change one block.

Once the structure is settled and length dominates, work block by block. A block is a contiguous group of tactics that turns one goal into its successor goals. Change one block per attempt and keep the rest of the best proof byte-identical so the measured difference is attributable. Lexical options that keep the proof's meaning:
- replace a final parenthesized argument with `$`: `f a (g (h x))` becomes `f a $ g $ h x`; this also applies to the last argument of a component inside ⟨…⟩ before `,`;
- when a term proves `x = e` for a local variable x, `cases` on that term substitutes it and is shorter than `rw [..]`;
- split `exact f a (g b)` into `apply f a` and `exact g b` when that removes brackets;
- drop parentheses that precedence does not require, such as `cases (A ▸ B)`;
- merge consecutive rewrites into one `rw [a, b]`; drop bullets when goal order already matches;
- a `have` pays off only if reused or needed in the context by a later `simp_all`/`assumption`; otherwise inline it.
These options depend on elaboration and must be confirmed by compilation.

When few attempts remain, complete and measure routes whose bridges are already validated. Repairs count as attempts; always preserve the best candidate. Record only validity, T, H, J, the acceptance decision and a brief justification.
